// Shot, Visuals, Dialogue, Duration.
:sceneColumns: Shot (Scene-Shot numbers, e.g. 1.1) | Visuals (what is seen, e.g. host, table, chart, etc.) | Dialogue (what the host says, or what is said as a v/o) | Duration (an estimate of the screen time)

= CloneZilla Recovery v0.5.1
> Create a disk image, and restore my system from it

== Cold Open: The Hook

// See {coldOpenScope} and {coldOpenTechniques} in the front matter
// for the scope of this section. The Hook is delivered within
// 15 seconds and is an integral part of the overall teaser, which
// runs for as long as it takes to engage the viewer and nail their
// attention.

== Scene 0, Scene Title: Cold Open (not seen on-screen)

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 0.1
| Host to camera, tight framing. Quick B-roll cut: a screen flickering, a file open dialog staring back.
| My hard drive died on a Tuesday. Everything I built, every document, every year of work - gone in one moment. Here is the one move that turns that disaster from the worst day of my life into a bad afternoon: **I save my whole disk as a single image with CloneZilla, and when disaster strikes, I restore that image onto a fresh disk and my system is back.** Today, I do both live on camera.
| 0:15

| 0.2
| Host to camera, relaxed.
| My name is Brian, I'm an AI Generalist, and this is DigitalCoreNZ
| 0:06
|===

== Title Sequence

Title card, animation, and music cue

**Duration:** 5-seconds

== Scene 1, Scene Title: One Bad Day

// The conflict beat: the setup and the problem.

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 1.1
| Host to camera.
| Let me paint the picture. I am three years into a project. My notebook runs an operating system, my tools, my files, my life. Then one day, it does not boot anymore. The disk is gone, and with it, everything on it.
| 0:12

| 1.2
| B-roll: a dead boot screen with a cursor blinking. Host back to camera.
| I can cry, I can curse - or I can do what I should have done all along. I take a complete picture of that disk while it is still alive: a disk image. One file. One snapshot of my whole system.
| 0:14

| 1.3
| Kinetic type: "One disk -> One image -> One restore".
| And when the worst day finally arrives, I drop that image onto a new disk, and I boot right back up. That is the whole trick. And the best part? I get it from CloneZilla, free, open-source software.
| 0:14
|===

== Scene 2, Scene Title: Boot the Rescue Disk

// Resolution beat one: prepare the tools.

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 2.1
| Host at the desk with a USB flash drive and a spare disk in hand.
| Here is my setup. I have my machine, and I have two extra disks. One is the disk I want to protect - that is disk one, sda in Linux. The other is a spare disk - sdb - that will live outside the machine and hold my image.
| 0:15

| 2.2
| B-roll: hand plugging the USB stick into the front of the PC; monitor powering on.
| I write the CloneZilla live image to a USB flash drive, and I plug that stick into the machine. Then I set the machine to boot from USB, and I power it on.
| 0:12

| 2.3
| Screen capture: the CloneZilla live boot menu.
| This is the boot menu. I choose the standard 800 by 600 mode, and I press Enter. I can watch Debian Linux booting underneath. This is my rescue environment, and it is all running from the USB stick.
| 0:14

| 2.4
| Screen capture: the language selection screen.
| First, it asks for my language. I pick mine.
| 0:06

| 2.5
| Screen capture: the keyboard layout screen.
| Next, my keyboard layout. I select the one that matches my country, so I type the way I expect to type.
| 0:08

| 2.6
| Screen capture: the CloneZilla main menu.
| Now I choose "Start Clonezilla". And CloneZilla opens in front of me. Everything I am about to do lives inside this one menu.
| 0:10
|===

== Scene 3, Scene Title: Save a Disk Image

// Resolution beat two: the savedisk walkthrough.

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 3.1
| Screen capture: the CloneZilla task menu. Host v/o.
| Time to make my image. In the task menu, I choose "device-image". That means I am working between a device - a disk - and an image.
| 0:10

| 3.2
| Screen capture: the image type menu.
| Next, I choose "local_dev". That tells CloneZilla my image home is a local device - in my case, a spare disk I have plugged into the machine.
| 0:10

| 3.3
| Screen capture: the image repository selection, sdb1 highlighted.
| Here I select sdb1 - a partition on my spare disk - as the image repository. This is where the image file physically lands. Get this right, because this is the only place my backup will exist.
| 0:13

| 3.4
| Screen capture: the task options.
| Now for the important one. I choose "savedisk". That is the instruction: save this whole disk as an image.
| 0:08

| 3.5
| Screen capture: the image name and source disk prompt.
| CloneZilla asks me for an image name. I give it something I will remember in the dark, something like "my-notebook-2026". Then it asks which disk to save, and I select sda - the disk I am protecting.
| 0:14

| 3.6
| Screen capture: the progress log scrolling.
| That is it. CloneZilla is now saving my disk image - sda - onto sdb1. I watch the log, and I let it run. Depending on the size of my disk and how fast my hardware is, this takes its time, so I step back.
| 0:12
|===

== Scene 4, Scene Title: The Warning

// Conflict re-entry: the stakes of the restore, before the restore happens.

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 4.1
| Host to camera, close framing, no music.
| Before I show you the other half of this video - the restore - I need to stop you and say the most important thing in the whole video.
| 0:08

| 4.2
| Kinetic type on a red background: "RESTORE = OVERWRITE".
| When I restore a system from an image, everything on the target partition or drive will be overwritten. No warning at the door. No recycle bin. No undo.
| 0:12

| 4.3
| Host to camera.
| That means I select the destination disk with care. If I point restoredisk at the wrong disk, I destroy that disk in minutes. So I check the disk names, I double-check the sizes, and I do not rush this step.
| 0:16

| 4.4
| Host to camera, then B-roll of the spare USB stick being unplugged.
| And one more rule I live by: after a restore that I rely on, I verify it works before I touch the old disk. Only when my new system boots and my data is there do I decide the failed disk is safe to retire.
| 0:15
|===

== Scene 5, Scene Title: Restore the System

// Resolution beat three: the restoredisk walkthrough.

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 5.1
| Host at the desk. B-roll: plugging in the USB stick and the spare image disk.
| Now for the pay-off. I have my machine, and I have my spare disk holding my image. I boot the machine from the same CloneZilla live USB stick.
| 0:12

| 5.2
| Screen capture: boot menu, language, keyboard layout, in quick succession.
| I press Enter at the 800 by 600 menu. I confirm my language. I confirm my keyboard layout. Same rescue environment as before.
| 0:12

| 5.3
| Screen capture: "Start Clonezilla" selected.
| I choose "Start Clonezilla", and I am in the main menu.
| 0:06

| 5.4
| Screen capture: "device-image" selected.
| Again, I choose "device-image". I am moving between a disk and an image, just like before.
| 0:08

| 5.5
| Screen capture: "local_dev" selected, then sdb1.
| I choose "local_dev", and I select sdb1 as the image repository - the very same partition where I saved my image. This is the side of the room my backup is living in.
| 0:13

| 5.6
| Screen capture: "restoredisk" highlighted.
| And now I choose "restoredisk". Read that one twice in your head: restore, disk. CloneZilla is about to erase a disk and rebuild it from my image.
| 0:12

| 5.7
| Screen capture: the image name list and destination disk selection.
| I select my image by name - "my-notebook-2026". And I select the destination disk. And this is the moment from the warning scene. One wrong letter, gone forever. I check the name, I check the size, and I confirm.
| 0:18

| 5.8
| Screen capture: the progress log, then a clean boot of the restored system.
| CloneZilla is restoring the disk image from my spare disk onto the target disk. It runs, it finishes, and I boot the machine from that restored disk. My desktop comes up. My files are there. My system is back.
| 0:14
|===

== Scene 6, Scene Title: In Review

// See {reviewScope} in the front matter for the scope of this
// section: a quick recap of the video, the CTA, and the Signature
// Sign Off, in that order.

|===
| Shot | Visuals | Dialogue | Duration

| 6.1
| Host to camera, kinetic type recapping the key menu path:
"device-image -> local_dev -> savedisk / restoredisk".
| Quick recap. I boot CloneZilla from a USB stick. I choose device-image, then local_dev, then a partition on my spare disk. If I want a backup, I choose savedisk, I name my image, and I pick the disk I am protecting. If the day has gone wrong, I choose restoredisk, I pick my image and my destination disk - carefully - and my system comes back to life. That is the whole loop: **I save my whole disk as one image with CloneZilla, and when disaster strikes, I restore that image onto a fresh disk and my system is back.**
| 0:28

| 6.2
| Host to camera.
| Click the Like button, and Subscribe for more content, if this is the kind of video you enjoy.
| 0:06

| 6.3
| Host to camera, warm and direct.
| Until next time: Be safe, be kind, be awesome, kia kaha!!
| 0:05
|===

